.Dd August 2, 2025
.Dt THREADING 3cutlet
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm threading
.Nd cutlet basic threading library
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb threading
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ic import Ar threading
.Pp
.Bl -inset -compact
.It Ic def Ar thread body
.It Ic def Ar mutex
.El
.Ss Variable Operators
.Bl -inset -compact
.It Ic $thread Ar join
.It Ic $thread Ar joinable
.It Ic $thread Ar detach
.Pp
.It Ic $mutex Ar body
.It Ic $mutex Ar try ¿timeout? body
.It Ic $mutex Ar shared body
.It Ic $mutex Ar try shared timeout body
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library adds very basic threading functionally to Cutlet.
.Ss Threading
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic def Ar thread body
Creates and starts a new thread that executes
.Ar body
and returns a thread variable.
.Bd -literal
local t1 = [thread {
  print Hello from thread
}]
.Ed
.It Ic $thread Ar join
Waits for a thread to complete and cleans up any resources used by the thread.
If an exception was thrown in the thread it will be rethrown when the thread
is joined.
.Pp
Note that if join is never called the garbage collector will attempt to join
the thread if possible.
This is not ideal and any exceptions thrown will be lost.
If you do not need to join the thread then
.Ar $thread detach
should be used instead of relying on the garbage collector.
.Bd -literal
try {
  $t1 join
} catch err {
  print "Thread raised error: $err"
}
.Ed
.It Ic $thread Ar joinable
Returns true if a thread is joinable.
Any thread that has already been joined or has been detached will
return false.
.It Ic $thread Ar detach
Detaches the thread from any controlling thread.
When a detached thread finishes all resources are automatically cleaned up and
there is no need to join it.
The
.Ar $thread joinable
will return false once the thread is detacted.
A detacted thread must handle any expections that are thrown as the controlling
thread no longer is able to receive them.
.Bd -literal
local t1 = [thread {
  ...
}]
$t1 detach
# Do not need to think about the thread again.
.Ed
.El
.Ss Access Control
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic def Ar mutex
Creates a mutex to control thread access to shared resources.
.Bd -literal
local mtx = [mutex]
.Ed
.It Ic $mutex Ar body
Only allow one thread at a time to execute
.Ar body .
This will wait indefinitely for a lock to be aquired and could be a possible
source of lock ups.
.Bd -literal
$mtx {
  print Hello from thread
}
.Ed
.It Ic $mutex Ar try ¿timeout? body
Only allow one thread at a time to execute
.Ar body
but if the lock cannot be aquired the operator returns false.
It will return true if the body was executed successfully.
.Pp
If the
.Ar timeout
argument is specified, in milliseconds, then the mutex will wait at least that
long before returning true or false.
.It Ic $mutex Ar shared body
This allows multiple threads to execute
.Ar body
at the same time.
This is used when the threads are only reading information and not making any
modifications and can improve performance.
When a shared resource needs to be modified then a full lock should be used.
This will wait indefinitely for a lock to be aquired and could be a possible
source of lock ups.
.Bd -literal
# Write lock
$mtx {
  global value = $new_value
}

# Read only lock
$mtx shared {
  print $value
}
.Ed
.It Ic $mutex Ar try shared timeout body
Try to obtain a shared lock or
.Ar timeout ,
which ever comes first.
The timeout value is in milliseconds.
.Bd -literal
local res = [$mtx try shared 300 {
  ...
}]
if {$res not} then {
  # A lock couldn't be obtained
  ...
}
.Ed
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cutlet 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Ron Wills
.Mt <ron@digitalcombine.ca>
